# Tropical Spectrum Duality for EML Function Algebras

This file establishes a tropical analog of the classical Gelfand–Kolmogorov duality:
a compact Hausdorff space `X` can be recovered (up to homeomorphism) from the
"evaluation spectrum" of a separating algebra of continuous real-valued functions.

## Main Results

* `evalCongr_injective` — evaluation kernel congruences separate points
* `preimage_tropVanishPair` — preimages of tropical vanishing loci are equalizer sets
* `isClosed_equalizer` — equalizer sets of continuous functions are closed
* `continuous_evalToSpec` — the evaluation-to-spectrum map is continuous
* `evalToSpec_surjective` — the evaluation-to-spectrum map is surjective
* `evaluation_homeomorph_tropMaxSpec` — the main homeomorphism theorem

## Mathematical Overview

In classical functional analysis, the Gelfand representation identifies a compact
Hausdorff space with the maximal ideal spectrum of its C*-algebra of continuous functions.
In the tropical (idempotent) setting, where addition is replaced by max and multiplication
by ordinary addition, the role of maximal ideals is played by *maximal congruences* —
equivalence relations compatible with the semiring operations.

The key insight is that each point `x ∈ X` determines a congruence on the function
algebra via `f ≡ g ↔ f(x) = g(x)` (the kernel of the evaluation homomorphism at `x`).
When the algebra *kernel-separates* points of `X` (for distinct x, y there exist f, g
with f(x) = g(x) but f(y) ≠ g(y)), this map is injective. By construction it is
surjective onto the evaluation spectrum. Combined with the compact-to-Hausdorff
homeomorphism criterion, this yields a homeomorphism between `X` and the
tropical evaluation spectrum.

